menu "WireGuard"

# esp_wireguard targets esp-idf >= 6.0 only. ESP-NETIF is the only
# supported TCP/IP adapter; the legacy TCP/IP Adapter (pre esp-idf
# v4.1, ESP8266 RTOS SDK) choice has been removed. CONFIG_WIREGUARD_ESP_NETIF
# is kept (always on) so existing #if defined(CONFIG_WIREGUARD_ESP_NETIF)
# guards elsewhere keep working.
config WIREGUARD_ESP_NETIF
    bool
    default y

# Work around a type-confusion bug between this component and esp_netif.
#
# This component registers a raw lwIP netif with netif_add(), bypassing
# esp_netif entirely (wg_netif_struct.state starts out pointing at a
# `struct wireguardif_init_data` on the caller's stack, and is later
# replaced by wireguardif_init() with a `struct wireguard_device *`; it
# is never an `esp_netif_t *`).
#
# netif_add() calls netif_set_addr() *before* invoking the netif's own
# init callback (wireguardif_init()). When a real (non-zero) IP address
# is assigned -- which esp_wireguard_netif_create() always does --
# netif_set_addr() fires lwIP's ext-callback chain with a
# LWIP_NSC_IPV4_*_CHANGED reason. esp_netif's netif_callback_fn()
# (components/esp_netif/lwip/esp_netif_lwip.c) is registered globally
# for *every* lwIP netif via netif_add_ext_callback(), so it also runs
# for this one. It reacts to that reason by calling
# lwip_get_esp_netif(netif), which, when LWIP_ESP_NETIF_DATA==0 (the
# esp-idf default), is simply `(esp_netif_t *)netif->state`.
#
# At the point netif_set_addr() runs, netif->state is still the
# `struct wireguardif_init_data *` that esp_wireguard_netif_create()
# put there -- not an esp_netif_t -- so esp_netif's callback
# dereferences it as one (esp_netif->ip_info, esp_netif->last_status_up,
# ...) and corrupts memory / crashes. This matches the netif_add crash
# reported against the upstream project.
#
# Selecting ESP_NETIF_BRIDGE_EN forces LWIP_ESP_NETIF_DATA to 1
# (see components/lwip/port/include/lwipopts.h), which makes
# lwip_get_esp_netif() read the esp-netif pointer from lwIP's
# `netif->client_data[]` array (via netif_get_client_data()) instead of
# overloading `netif->state`. Since this WireGuard netif was never
# registered through esp_netif, its client_data slot is simply NULL,
# lwip_get_esp_netif() returns NULL, and esp_netif's callback safely
# no-ops (`if (esp_netif) { ... }`) instead of misinterpreting
# netif->state.
#
# This is a minimal, build-verified workaround, not a "real" fix: it
# piggybacks on a Kconfig option named for an unrelated feature (the
# lwIP bridge) purely for its side effect on LWIP_ESP_NETIF_DATA, and it
# does not make this netif an actual esp_netif (events such as
# IP_EVENT_STA_GOT_IP still won't fire for it; use the peer-up callback
# / esp_wireguardif_peer_is_up() instead). Registering this interface
# as a proper esp_netif with a custom driver would be the "correct" fix,
# and remains a possible future improvement (see CHANGES-fork.md).
#
# NOTE: this is a compile-time/config fix, verified only by inspecting
# the esp-idf 6.0.2 source (components/esp_netif/lwip/esp_netif_lwip.c,
# components/lwip/port/include/lwipopts.h) and by a clean rebuild of
# examples/demo. It has NOT been verified on real hardware.
config WIREGUARD_WORKAROUND_ESP_NETIF_TYPE_CONFUSION
    bool
    default y
    select ESP_NETIF_BRIDGE_EN

config WIREGUARD_MAX_PEERS
    int "Max number of peers"
    default 1

config WIREGUARD_MAX_SRC_IPS
	int "Max number of SRC IP addresses"
	default 2

config MAX_INITIATIONS_PER_SECOND
    int "Per device limit on accepting (valid) initiation requests"
    default 2
    help
        Per device limit on accepting (valid) initiation requests - per peer.

config WIREGUARD_TIME_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "Milliseconds to wait for a plausible clock before connecting"
    default 30000
    help
        The ESP32 has no battery-backed RTC, so its clock reads near the
        Unix epoch after every reboot until something (normally SNTP) sets
        it. WireGuard's handshake replay protection requires each peer's
        initiation timestamp to strictly increase, so sending a handshake
        before the clock is set risks sending a timestamp lower than one a
        peer has already seen, and being silently rejected as a replay.

        esp_wireguard_connect() polls wireguard_platform_time_is_synced()
        and waits (in CONFIG_WIREGUARD_TIME_SYNC_POLL_INTERVAL_MS steps)
        for up to this long before giving up and returning ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT,
        instead of sending a handshake with an unset clock. It does not
        start SNTP itself; the application is still responsible for that
        (see examples/demo/main/sync_time.c) -- this only waits for it to
        finish.

config WIREGUARD_TIME_SYNC_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Milliseconds between clock checks while waiting to connect"
    default 500
    help
        How often esp_wireguard_connect() re-checks the clock while
        waiting for it to become plausible. See
        CONFIG_WIREGUARD_TIME_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS.

config WIREGUARD_MTU
    int "MTU of the WireGuard interface"
    default 1420
    help
        1420 is the same default used by wg-quick and most WireGuard
        implementations: it fits a full-size (1500-byte MTU) WireGuard
        UDP packet (60 bytes of IPv4 + UDP + WireGuard transport-data
        overhead) without IP fragmentation, with a little headroom left
        for paths with extra encapsulation (PPPoE, some VPN-over-VPN or
        tunneled setups) that would otherwise need a lower MTU to avoid
        fragmenting the *outer* UDP packet.

        Lower this if the path between this device and its peer has a
        smaller MTU than a typical Ethernet link -- the most common
        real-world case being a cellular (LTE/5G) data connection, which
        can be as low as ~1380-1400 bytes end-to-end once the carrier's
        own encapsulation is accounted for. There is no single correct
        value for every network; this should be set from a measurement
        of the actual path (e.g. `ping -M do -s <size> <peer>` from the
        remote end to find the largest non-fragmenting packet, or by
        checking for retransmits/stalls under load with the MTU at its
        default), not changed speculatively. See CHANGES-fork.md, Phase
        4, for this fork's own measurement notes once hardware testing
        of this is done.

        Changing this does not change the WireGuard protocol framing
        overhead this value accounts for (60 bytes, fixed by the
        protocol) -- it only changes what's reported to lwIP as the
        tunnel interface's MTU, which TCP's MSS is derived from, so
        setting it too high for the real path risks fragmentation (or,
        if an intermediate hop drops fragments as cellular/CGNAT paths
        sometimes do, delivery failures that are hard to diagnose) and
        setting it too low under-uses the available path MTU.
config WIREGUARD_MONITOR_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Monitor task poll interval (ms)"
    default 1000
    help
        How often the background task started by esp_wireguard_start_monitor()
        (Phase 5) calls esp_wireguardif_peer_is_up() to detect up/down
        transitions and post WIREGUARD_EVENT_PEER_UP / _PEER_DOWN
        accordingly. The underlying wireguardif/wireguard.c library has no
        up/down callback of its own, so this is still polling under the
        hood -- what changes is who does the polling: the component now
        does it once, internally, so the application can subscribe to
        events instead of running its own poll loop.

        Lower values detect a dropped peer sooner, at the cost of waking
        the CPU (and, on a WiFi power-save device, the radio) more often.
        The WireGuard protocol's own keepalive/handshake-retry timers
        already operate on the order of several seconds to tens of
        seconds, so values much below 500ms rarely detect a real
        transition any sooner in practice -- they just poll more often in
        between.

choice WIREGUARD_x25519_IMPLEMENTATION
    prompt "x25519 implementation to use"
    default WIREGUARD_x25519_IMPLEMENTATION_DEFAULT
    config WIREGUARD_x25519_IMPLEMENTATION_DEFAULT
        bool "Default (originally from wireguard-lwip)"
    config WIREGUARD_x25519_IMPLEMENTATION_NACL
        bool "NaCL"
endchoice
endmenu
